---
import Post from "../../../layouts/Post.astro";
import RecordDisclosure from "../../../components/RecordDisclosure.astro";
import bests from "../../../data/running/personal-bests.json";
const frontmatter = {
  title: "Running record",
  description: "A lifelong running project: current training, progress, personal bests and the full dated history.",
  updated: "2026-10-26",
};
const selectedBests = ["best-800", "best-1500", "best-5000-track"].map((id) => bests.rows.find((best) => best.id === id));
---
<Post frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p>
    I am rebuilding middle-distance training, with <a href="/running-notebook/#the-runner-i-am-trying-to-build">800+ World
    Athletics points across 800–5000 m</a> as a checkpoint in a lifelong project.
    The record follows what I try, what happens and how the training changes.
  </p>
  <p class="overview-nav">
    <a href="/running-notebook/">Notebook</a>{" · "}
    <a href="/running/record/2026/programme/">Programme</a>{" · "}
    <a href="/running/record/results/#personal-bests">Personal bests</a>{" · "}
    <a href="#history">History</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="current-training">Current training</h2>
  <p class="as-of">Latest completed session in this record: 23 October 2026</p>
  <p>
    The current cycle combines repeatable LT, VO₂ and fast sessions. The longer
    repetitions are now <strong>3×1600 m and 3×900 m</strong>, after independently
    earning their next steps. The latest LT session was quicker and more composed;
    ankle tension afterwards keeps the next session conditional on recovery.
    The <a href="/running-notebook/#what-the-observations-now-tell-me">notebook</a>{" "}
    explains the decisions; the <a href="/running/record/2026/programme/">programme</a>{" "}
    retains the planned cycle and progression gates.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recent-sessions">Recent sessions</h2>
  <ul class="recent-sessions">
    <li>
      <a href="/running/record/2026/#23-october--lt-31600-m--nominal-1-min"><strong>23 October</strong> · LT, 3×1600 m</a>
      <span>6:18.46 average · quicker closing laps and good posture; ankle tension afterwards.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/running/record/2026/#21-october--fa-4200-m--3-min--4200-m--90-seconds"><strong>21 October</strong> · mixed-rest 4+4×200 m</a>
      <span>About 30.41 per 200 m · speed held through increasing stiffness; one longer rep converted to 200 m.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/running/record/2026/#19-october--vo-3900-m--nominal-530"><strong>19 October</strong> · VO₂, 3×900 m</a>
      <span>2:57.00 average · smooth opening, then heavy legs and a difficult headwind.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href="/running/record/2026/#repeating-a-protocol">Compare repeated workouts</a>{" · "}
    <a href="/running/record/2026/#the-completed-sessions">All current sessions</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="selected-bests">Selected best marks</h2>
  <dl class="selected-bests">
    {selectedBests.map((best) => <div>
      <dt><a href={`/running/record/results/#${best.id}`}>{best.distance}</a></dt>
      <dd><strong>{best.time}</strong><span>{best.kind} · {best.date.slice(0, 4)}</span></dd>
    </div>)}
  </dl>
  <p>
    These marks come from different years and settings. The{" "}
    <a href="/running/record/results/#personal-bests">full personal-best table</a>{" "}
    keeps dates, paces, points and measurement context together; the{" "}
    <a href="/running/record/results/#race-history">race history</a>{" "}
    retains every recovered result.
  </p>

  <h2 id="history">History</h2>
  <p>
    Each period opens with its story and selected dated sessions. Monthly lists
    underneath retain the individual activity uploads.
  </p>
  <details class="history-index">
  <summary>Browse years: 2015–2026</summary>
  <h3 id="periods">Periods</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2026/">2026: returning to the track</a> —
      calibration, repeatable sessions and the independently earned LT and VO₂ progressions.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2023-2025/">2023–2025: running beyond the track season</a> —
      intermittent training, the Orange County Marathon and later long runs.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2022/">2022: the track season</a> —
      the recovered programme, time trials and men's and U20 national championships.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2021/">2021: the 900s and the rest of the season</a> —
      twelve iterations of a familiar workout, the South Park 5 km best and the running between them.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2020/">2020: short tests, track laps and longer runs</a> —
      summer marks, mixed-pace sessions, the Sofia short race and longer road outings.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/early/">2015–2019: the early record</a> —
      the neighbourhood loop, organized park races and the first exported runs.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>
  <h2 id="reading-beyond-the-record">Reading beyond the record</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/running-notebook/">800–5000 m: a running notebook</a> — goals,
      coaching decisions and the observations that change them.</li>
    <li><a href="/what-should-a-running-workout-maximize/">What should a running workout
      maximize?</a> — the questions behind the training.</li>
    <li><a href="/running-makes-the-city-smaller/">Running makes the city smaller</a> —
      the freedom to move through a city on foot.</li>
  </ul>

  <details class="record-method">
  <summary id="how-to-read-a-record">How to read a record</summary>
  <p>
    Race results come from organizers or the athletics federation. Training times
    retain the repetition distance, recovery and circumstances recorded with them.
    A remembered effort keeps its uncertainty; a GPS distance remains a device
    measurement. These distinctions matter when two similar numbers describe
    different kinds of running.
  </p>
  <p>
    Longer workout notes give pace per kilometre alongside the repetition times,
    using the stated work distance. Short fast repetitions retain their splits.
    The monthly lists show each recording's average moving pace, which can include
    recoveries. Hills, surface and distance uncertainty stay beside the
    pace wherever they affect the comparison. The notebook's{" "}
    <a href="/running-notebook/#the-runner-i-am-trying-to-build">800-point goal profile</a>{" "}
    supplies the common event standard behind the training.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where climbing matters, the notes include recorded elevation gain or the
    known route profile. A gain total alone cannot establish an adjusted pace:
    how the climbs and descents are distributed matters too.{" "}
    <a href="https://support.strava.com/en-us/articles/15402117-what-is-grade-adjusted-pace-gap-on-strava">Strava's
    grade-adjusted pace</a> estimates a flat-equivalent pace from steepness and
    does not account for technical terrain or its condition. These records
    retain actual pace and terrain context; no adjusted values have been
    reconstructed from elevation totals.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each period opens with the story and selected dated details. Beneath them,
    monthly lists preserve all 375 running activity uploads in the supplied history,
    through 8 October 2026 locally. Warm-ups and cool-downs can have their own
    recordings; several uploads can belong to one outing. The lists therefore
    count recordings rather than physical training sessions. Earlier races and
    unrecorded memories can still have a place here.
  </p>
  <p>
    This history reaches from childhood park races to the present return to the
    track, with the workouts, longer outings and memories that connect them.
    The <a href="/running-notebook/">notebook</a> holds the active project; these
    pages retain what happened as that project changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    New sessions will join the relevant period. A substantial change in the
    training can begin another chapter; an ordinary month or completed cycle can
    remain inside one. The programme that governed a session stays identifiable
    after the next revision.
  </p>
  </details>
</Post>
<RecordDisclosure />
<style>
  .overview-nav { margin: 22px 0 32px; }
  .as-of { color: var(--dim); font-size: 12px; margin-top: -12px; }
  .recent-sessions { list-style: none; padding-left: 0; }
  .recent-sessions li { margin-bottom: 14px; }
  .recent-sessions span { display: block; color: var(--dim); margin-top: 3px; }
  .selected-bests { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin: 20px 0; }
  .selected-bests div { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 10px; }
  .selected-bests dt { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .selected-bests dd { margin: 0; }
  .selected-bests dd strong { font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; }
  .selected-bests dd span { display: block; color: var(--dim); font-size: 12px; margin-top: 3px; }
  .history-index summary { cursor: pointer; }
  .record-method { margin-top: 32px; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 16px; }
  .record-method summary { cursor: pointer; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .selected-bests { gap: 10px; }
    .selected-bests dd strong { font-size: 17px; }
  }
</style>
